This project, `haskell-halogen-router`, is based on code from the PureScript routing-duplex
library, licensed under MIT, and adapted to GHC Haskell.
See https://github.com/natefaubion/purescript-routing-duplex for the original project
(Copyright (c) 2018 Nathan Faubion).

`Halogen.Router.Duplex` follows its `Routing.Duplex`, `Routing.Duplex.Parser` and
`Routing.Duplex.Printer`: a codec is a printer and a parser built side by side, an address is
taken apart into segments, query parameters and a fragment, and the combinators (`root`, `path`,
`prefix`, `suffix`, `segment`, `param`, `flag`, `int`, `string`, `boolean`, `as`, `optional`,
`many`, `many1`, `rest`, `end`) keep their names and meaning. `Halogen.Router.Duplex.Generic`
follows `Routing.Duplex.Generic`.

The history half, `Halogen.Router`, is modelled on the PushState and Hash interfaces of a second
project, licensed under Apache 2.0:
- https://github.com/purescript-contrib/purescript-routing

It builds on `haskell-halogen-core`, which carries its own NOTICE covering the PureScript
projects it was adapted from.

The implementation differs where Haskell differs. The codec of a sum type is derived with
`GHC.Generics`, from a type-indexed list of codecs labelled with the constructors' names
(`#Home .= noArgs .| #Post .= …`), where the original reads a PureScript record through `Generic`
and `RowToList`; a constructor takes at most one argument, and a missing codec or a label that
names no constructor is a type error. A route is read only when the
whole path has been consumed, and the parser backtracks over every reading of an address rather
than committing to the first alternative that succeeds, so a route that is a prefix of another
does not shadow it; a failed parse reports the failure that got furthest. Records of route parts
are built with an `Applicative` instance and `lmap` rather than the original's record combinators,
and there are no operators. The router keeps the address in memory natively, so that a page can
be rendered for any route without a browser.

